config PARAVIRT_SCHED_HOST
	tristate "Paravirt scheduling host framework"
	depends on KVM
	help
	  Paravirtualized scheduling lets the host and a guest exchange
	  scheduling information through a shared memory region, so the
	  hypervisor can schedule vCPU threads more effectively -- for
	  example, boosting the priority of a vCPU thread while the guest
	  runs a latency-sensitive workload on it.

	  This option enables the host side framework, which attaches to KVM's
	  vCPU scheduling tracepoints and dispatches their events to a policy.
	  The policy itself is supplied as a kernel module or a BPF program.
	  Built as a module (pvsched), which depends on KVM.

	  If unsure, say N.

config PVSCHED_DEMO
	tristate "Paravirt scheduling demo policy (testing)"
	depends on PARAVIRT_SCHED_HOST
	help
	  A minimal sample pvsched policy module used to exercise the host
	  framework end to end. It registers a policy named "demo" that applies
	  the guest's requested scheduling parameters to the vCPU thread on
	  vmexit, publishes the vCPU status in the shared page, boosts the vCPU
	  on interrupt injection, and exposes event counters in debugfs
	  (pvsched_demo/stats).

	  If unsure, say N.

config PARAVIRT_SCHED_GUEST
	bool "Paravirt scheduling guest support"
	depends on X86_64 && TRACEPOINTS
	help
	  The guest core of paravirtualized scheduling: owns the shared
	  per-vCPU pages, negotiates a policy with the host, and publishes the
	  guest's scheduling intent (the parameters of the task each vCPU
	  runs) for the host policy to act on. Built-in because the publishing
	  hooks attach to unexported core tracepoints. A transport driver
	  (e.g. PVSCHED_GUEST_PCI) is needed to reach the VMM.

	  A kernel with this enabled runs unchanged on a host without pvsched:
	  with no transport (or no host policy match) the hooks stay patched
	  out.

	  If unsure, say N.

config PVSCHED_GUEST_PCI
	tristate "Paravirt scheduling guest PCI transport"
	depends on PARAVIRT_SCHED_GUEST && PCI
	help
	  The PCI binding of the pvsched discovery transport: a thin driver
	  that binds the pvsched device exposed by the VMM and hands the guest
	  core's shared pages to it through the device's table and doorbell.

	  If unsure, say N.
